  "testStepWeightsNote": [
    "Milliseconds per step, from one sequential run of the whole suite. Only the",
    "shard packing in tools/run-tests.mjs reads them, plus run-tests-parallel.mjs,",
    "which divides by their sum for its speedup figure. No test behaviour depends",
    "on them: a step missing from this table is packed at the median, not skipped.",
    "",
    "A SNAPSHOT, NOT A CONSTANT. Measured 2026-09-20/21, 122 steps, 856.4s total, on",
    "one 4-core box under one load. Both halves of that drift: steps get slower as",
    "they grow, and the whole table shifts with the machine's contention. So these",
    "numbers go stale on their own, with nothing broken and nothing to find.",
    "",
    "What that looks like in practice, from this refresh: the previous table was",
    "within ~3% in TOTAL (768.3s against a real 760.4s) yet individual steps were",
    "off by up to 3x - library:save 200 -> 68, library:browser 1800 -> 3800. The",
    "per-step error is the part that matters. It is invisible in the sum and it is",
    "what pulls the shards out of balance: repacking on these numbers took the",
    "slowest shard from 135.3s to 126.7s and the spread across six from 16.5s to 0.",
    "",
    "So treat drift as maintenance, not a bug. The symptom is uneven shard wall",
    "times, never a failing test. Re-measure by running the suite sequentially",
    "and rewriting the block below from the per-step times; `node",
    "tools/run-tests.mjs --shard-plan 6` prints the packing they produce.",
    "",
    "And do not read a single test:par as a trend. That speedup divides this",
    "table's sum by a contended wall clock, so it swings on load alone: two",
    "consecutive runs here, on ONE unchanged table, measured 1.73x (445.2s wall)",
    "and 1.53x (500.9s). A low number on its own is noise, not drift - which is",
    "the easiest way to mistake this for a regression. Compare several runs, and",
    "judge the PACKING with --shard-plan, which has no wall clock in it at all."
  ],
